DATA={'V/01': {'intro': 'Commutative algebra begins with rings, ideals, and quotients. Ideals are exactly the additive subgroups that can serve as kernels of ring homomorphisms, and quotient rings convert congruence conditions into algebraic objects.', 'sections': [('Commutative rings', 'A commutative ring has associative addition and multiplication, a multiplicative identity, and commutative multiplication.'), ('Ideals', 'An ideal is an additive subgroup stable under multiplication by arbitrary ring elements.'), ('Generated ideals', 'The ideal generated by elements $a_1,\\dots,a_n$ consists of finite combinations $r_1a_1+\\cdots+r_na_n$.'), ('Sums and intersections', 'Sums give the smallest ideal containing two ideals, while intersections give their greatest common subideal.'), ('Quotient rings', 'For an ideal $I\\subset A$, the quotient $A/I$ has cosets as elements and multiplication induced from $A$.'), ('Ring homomorphisms', 'Kernels are ideals and images are subrings; quotienting by the kernel isolates the injective part of a map.'), ('Correspondence of ideals', 'Ideals of $A/I$ correspond to ideals of $A$ containing $I$.'), ('Examples', 'Polynomial rings, integer residue rings, coordinate-style quotients, and product rings provide the main laboratory.')], 'theorems': [('First isomorphism theorem', 'For a ring homomorphism $\\varphi:A\\to B$, there is a natural isomorphism $A/\\ker\\varphi\\cong\\operatorname{im}\\varphi$.', 'Send the class of $a$ to $\\varphi(a)$. Equality of images is exactly equality modulo the kernel, so the map is well-defined and injective; surjectivity onto the image is immediate.'), ('Correspondence theorem', 'Ideals of $A/I$ are in inclusion-preserving bijection with ideals $J\\subset A$ satisfying $I\\subseteq J$.', 'Take inverse image under the quotient map in one direction and quotient $J/I$ in the other. The two constructions are inverse by direct inspection.'), ('Third isomorphism theorem', 'If $I\\subseteq J\\subseteq A$ are ideals, then $(A/I)/(J/I)\\cong A/J$.', 'Apply the first isomorphism theorem to the homomorphism $A/I\\to A/J$ sending $a+I$ to $a+J$; its kernel is $J/I$.')]}, 'V/02': {'intro': 'Prime and maximal ideals encode the irreducible quotient structures of a commutative ring. Prime ideals detect domains, maximal ideals detect fields, and maximal ideals are automatically prime.', 'sections': [('Prime ideals', 'A proper ideal $\\mathfrak p$ is prime when $ab\\in\\mathfrak p$ implies $a\\in\\mathfrak p$ or $b\\in\\mathfrak p$.'), ('Maximal ideals', 'A proper ideal $\\mathfrak m$ is maximal when no proper ideal lies strictly between $\\mathfrak m$ and the whole ring.'), ('Quotient criteria', 'Prime ideals correspond to domain quotients; maximal ideals correspond to field quotients.'), ('Existence of maximal ideals', "Every proper ideal lies in a maximal ideal, using Zorn's lemma."), ('Contraction', 'Inverse images of prime ideals under ring maps are prime when the inverse image remains proper.'), ('Prime chains', 'Chains of prime ideals reflect increasing algebraic specialization and later feed into dimension theory.'), ('Units and maximal ideals', 'An element is a unit iff it belongs to no maximal ideal.'), ('Examples', 'Prime and maximal ideals in $\\mathbb Z$, polynomial rings, product rings, and localizations illustrate the definitions.')], 'theorems': [('Prime quotient criterion', 'A proper ideal $\\mathfrak p$ is prime iff $A/\\mathfrak p$ is an integral domain.', 'The zero-divisor condition in the quotient is exactly the statement that $ab\\in\\mathfrak p$ forces one factor into $\\mathfrak p$.'), ('Maximal quotient criterion', 'A proper ideal $\\mathfrak m$ is maximal iff $A/\\mathfrak m$ is a field.', 'Ideals in $A/\\mathfrak m$ correspond to ideals of $A$ containing $\\mathfrak m$. A quotient is a field exactly when its only ideals are zero and the whole ring.'), ('Maximal implies prime', 'Every maximal ideal in a commutative ring with identity is prime.', 'The quotient by a maximal ideal is a field, hence a domain; apply the prime quotient criterion.')]}, 'V/03': {'intro': 'Radicals and nilpotents measure how equations can vanish after taking powers. The radical of an ideal forgets multiplicity-like information and retains the underlying prime behavior.', 'sections': [('Nilpotent elements', 'An element $a$ is nilpotent if $a^n=0$ for some positive integer $n$.'), ('Nilradical', 'The nilradical $\\sqrt{(0)}$ is the ideal of all nilpotent elements.'), ('Radical of an ideal', '$\\sqrt I=\\{a:a^n\\in I\\text{ for some }n\\ge1\\}$.'), ('Radical ideals', 'An ideal is radical when $a^n\\in I$ implies $a\\in I$.'), ('Quotient interpretation', '$\\sqrt I/I$ is the nilradical of $A/I$.'), ('Prime containment', 'The radical of an ideal is the intersection of all prime ideals containing it.'), ('Reduced rings', 'A ring is reduced when its nilradical is zero.'), ('Examples', 'Powers of principal ideals, square-zero extensions, and polynomial quotients show how radicals discard nilpotent thickening.')], 'theorems': [('Radical is an ideal', 'For every ideal $I$, the set $\\sqrt I$ is an ideal containing $I$.', 'Closure under multiplication is immediate. If $a^m,b^n\\in I$, then every term in $(a+b)^{m+n}$ contains either at least $m$ copies of $a$ or at least $n$ copies of $b$, hence lies in $I$.'), ('Quotient nilradical identity', 'The nilradical of $A/I$ is $\\sqrt I/I$.', 'The class of $a$ is nilpotent modulo $I$ exactly when some power $a^n$ lies in $I$.'), ('Radical as intersection of primes', '$\\sqrt I$ equals the intersection of all prime ideals containing $I$.', 'One inclusion follows because primes are radical. For the reverse, if $a\\notin\\sqrt I$, localize the quotient $A/I$ at powers of $a$ and choose a maximal ideal there; its contraction is a prime containing $I$ but avoiding $a$.')]}, 'V/04': {'intro': 'Chinese remainder theory decomposes rings modulo comaximal ideals into products. It is both a structural theorem and an explicit congruence-solving method.', 'sections': [('Comaximal ideals', 'Ideals $I,J$ are comaximal when $I+J=A$.'), ('Two-ideal CRT', 'For comaximal $I,J$, the quotient by $I\\cap J$ maps isomorphically to $A/I\\times A/J$.'), ('Product equals intersection', 'For comaximal ideals, $I\\cap J=IJ$.'), ('Finite families', 'Pairwise comaximal finite families give product decompositions modulo the intersection or product.'), ('Explicit idempotents', 'CRT decompositions produce orthogonal idempotents selecting the different factors.'), ('Integer congruences', 'The classical integer CRT is the special case of principal ideals in $\\mathbb Z$.'), ('Polynomial interpolation form', 'Comaximal polynomial ideals encode simultaneous residue conditions.'), ('Decomposition philosophy', 'Product rings are algebraic manifestations of independent components.')], 'theorems': [('Two-ideal Chinese remainder theorem', 'If $I+J=A$, then $A/(I\\cap J)\\cong A/I\\times A/J$.', 'The natural map has kernel $I\\cap J$. For surjectivity choose $u\\in I,v\\in J$ with $u+v=1$; the element $bv+au$ has prescribed residues $a$ modulo $I$ and $b$ modulo $J$.'), ('Intersection-product equality', 'If $I+J=A$, then $I\\cap J=IJ$.', 'The inclusion $IJ\\subseteq I\\cap J$ is automatic. If $x\\in I\\cap J$ and $1=u+v$ with $u\\in I,v\\in J$, then $x=xu+xv$ lies in $IJ$.'), ('Finite CRT', 'If $I_1,\\dots,I_n$ are pairwise comaximal, then $A/(I_1\\cdots I_n)\\cong\\prod_i A/I_i$.', 'Induct on $n$, using that the product of the first $n-1$ ideals is comaximal with $I_n$, then apply the two-ideal theorem and the product-intersection identity.')]}, 'V/05': {'intro': 'Multiplicative systems specify which elements are to become invertible. They are the input data for localization and control which ideals, primes, and module elements survive.', 'sections': [('Multiplicative subsets', 'A multiplicative set $S$ contains $1$ and is closed under products; usually $0\\notin S$ for nontrivial localization.'), ('Saturation', 'The saturation of $S$ consists of elements that become invertible whenever all of $S$ does.'), ('Examples', 'Powers of one element, complements of prime ideals, and complements of unions of primes are standard multiplicative sets.'), ('Disjointness from ideals', 'An ideal disjoint from $S$ can survive as a proper ideal after localization.'), ('Prime avoidance viewpoint', 'Maximal ideals among ideals disjoint from $S$ are prime.'), ('Denominator calculus', 'Fractions identify numerators after multiplying by suitable elements of $S$.'), ('Universal goal', 'Localization is characterized by making $S$ invertible with no extra relations beyond those forced.'), ('Geometric preview', 'Choosing $S=\\{1,f,f^2,\\dots\\}$ corresponds to restricting attention to where $f$ is invertible.')], 'theorems': [('Maximal disjoint ideal is prime', 'If $I$ is maximal among ideals disjoint from a multiplicative set $S$, then $I$ is prime.', 'If $ab\\in I$ with $a,b\\notin I$, then both $I+(a)$ and $I+(b)$ meet $S$. Multiplying corresponding elements of $S$ yields an element of $I+(ab)\\subseteq I$, contradicting disjointness.'), ('Complement of prime is multiplicative', 'If $\\mathfrak p$ is prime, then $A\\setminus\\mathfrak p$ is multiplicatively closed.', 'If $a,b\\notin\\mathfrak p$ but $ab\\in\\mathfrak p$, primality would force one factor into $\\mathfrak p$.'), ('Powers form a multiplicative set', 'For any $f\\in A$, $S=\\{1,f,f^2,\\dots\\}$ is multiplicative.', 'The product $f^m f^n=f^{m+n}$ remains in the set and $1=f^0$ is included.')]}, 'V/06': {'intro': 'Localization of rings adjoins inverses to a chosen multiplicative system. It is governed by a universal property and induces a precise correspondence between primes of the localization and primes of the original ring disjoint from the denominators.', 'sections': [('Fraction construction', 'Elements of $S^{-1}A$ are fractions $a/s$ modulo the relation generated by cross-multiplication after multiplying by a further denominator.'), ('Ring operations', 'Fractions add and multiply by the usual formulas.'), ('Canonical map', 'The map $A\\to S^{-1}A$ sends $a$ to $a/1$ and makes every element of $S$ invertible.'), ('Universal property', 'Any ring map from $A$ sending $S$ to units factors uniquely through $S^{-1}A$.'), ('Zero criterion', 'A fraction $a/s$ is zero iff some $t\\in S$ annihilates $a$.'), ('Localized ideals', 'Extension sends $I$ to $S^{-1}I$, while contraction returns ideals saturated with respect to $S$.'), ('Prime correspondence', 'Prime ideals of $S^{-1}A$ correspond to prime ideals of $A$ disjoint from $S$.'), ('Localization at an element', '$A_f$ is obtained by inverting powers of one element $f$.'), ('Localization at a prime', '$A_{\\mathfrak p}$ inverts everything outside $\\mathfrak p$ and is a local ring.')], 'theorems': [('Universal property of localization', 'If $\\varphi:A\\to B$ sends every $s\\in S$ to a unit, there is a unique map $\\widetilde\\varphi:S^{-1}A\\to B$ with $\\widetilde\\varphi(a/s)=\\varphi(a)\\varphi(s)^{-1}$.', 'The formula respects the localization equivalence relation and is forced by the requirement that denominators become inverses, giving existence and uniqueness.'), ('Prime correspondence', 'Prime ideals of $S^{-1}A$ are in bijection with prime ideals $\\mathfrak p\\subset A$ satisfying $\\mathfrak p\\cap S=\\varnothing$.', 'Contraction of a prime avoids units, hence avoids $S$. For a prime disjoint from $S$, its extension consists of fractions with numerator in the prime and is prime; extension and contraction are inverse.'), ('Localization commutes with quotient', 'For an ideal $I$, $S^{-1}(A/I)\\cong S^{-1}A/S^{-1}I$ after replacing $S$ by its image in $A/I$.', 'Both rings satisfy the same universal property: maps out correspond to maps from $A$ killing $I$ and inverting $S$.')]}, 'V/07': {'intro': 'Localization of modules extends fraction methods from rings to linear algebra over rings. It is exact, commutes with finite algebraic constructions, and detects local behavior of modules.', 'sections': [('Module fractions', '$S^{-1}M$ consists of symbols $m/s$ modulo the expected denominator equivalence.'), ('Scalar structure', '$S^{-1}M$ is naturally a module over $S^{-1}A$.'), ('Universal property', 'Maps from $M$ into modules where $S$ acts invertibly factor uniquely through $S^{-1}M$.'), ('Tensor description', 'There is a natural isomorphism $S^{-1}M\\cong S^{-1}A\\otimes_A M$.'), ('Exactness', 'Localization preserves exact sequences.'), ('Submodules and quotients', 'Localization commutes with quotients and sends submodules to localized submodules.'), ('Vanishing criterion', 'An element $m/1$ vanishes locally iff some denominator in $S$ annihilates $m$.'), ('Finite generation', 'Localization preserves finite generation and finite presentation.'), ('Local detection', 'Many module properties can be tested after localizing at all prime or maximal ideals.')], 'theorems': [('Tensor description of localization', 'There is a natural isomorphism $S^{-1}A\\otimes_A M\\cong S^{-1}M$ sending $(a/s)\\otimes m$ to $am/s$.', 'The formula is balanced and induces a homomorphism. Its inverse sends $m/s$ to $(1/s)\\otimes m$; the two composites are identities.'), ('Exactness of localization', "If $0\\to M'\\to M\\to M''\\to0$ is exact, then $0\\to S^{-1}M'\\to S^{-1}M\\to S^{-1}M''\\to0$ is exact.", 'Surjectivity is immediate on fractions. For injectivity or kernel equality, if a localized element maps to zero, some denominator annihilates the relevant numerator, allowing the numerator to be moved into the preceding module before localization.'), ('Localization commutes with quotient', 'For $N\\subset M$, $S^{-1}(M/N)\\cong S^{-1}M/S^{-1}N$.', 'Apply exactness to $0\\to N\\to M\\to M/N\\to0$.')]}, 'V/08': {'intro': 'Local rings concentrate algebra around one maximal ideal. Localization at a prime produces the canonical local ring attached to that prime, and unit tests become especially simple.', 'sections': [('Local rings', 'A local ring has exactly one maximal ideal.'), ('Nonunits', 'In a local ring, the nonunits form the unique maximal ideal.'), ('Unit criterion', 'An element is a unit precisely when it lies outside the maximal ideal.'), ('Localization at primes', 'For a prime $\\mathfrak p$, $A_{\\mathfrak p}$ has maximal ideal $\\mathfrak p A_{\\mathfrak p}$.'), ('Residue field', 'The field $A_{\\mathfrak p}/\\mathfrak p A_{\\mathfrak p}$ is the fraction field of $A/\\mathfrak p$ with respect to nonzero classes.'), ('Nakayama preview', 'Local rings make finite-generation arguments rigid because the maximal ideal controls reduction modulo the residue field.'), ('Local homomorphisms', 'A homomorphism of local rings is local when the maximal ideal pulls back to the maximal ideal.'), ('Examples', 'Discrete valuation rings, localized polynomial rings, and power-series rings provide standard local examples.'), ('Local-global philosophy', 'Properties of modules and ideals are often checked prime by prime using these local rings.')], 'theorems': [('Nonunits characterize local rings', 'A commutative ring is local iff its nonunits form an ideal; in that case this ideal is the unique maximal ideal.', 'Every proper ideal consists of nonunits. If nonunits form an ideal, it is proper and contains every proper ideal, hence is uniquely maximal. Conversely, in a local ring every element outside the maximal ideal generates the unit ideal and is therefore a unit.'), ('Prime localization is local', 'For a prime $\\mathfrak p$, the ring $A_{\\mathfrak p}$ is local with unique maximal ideal $\\mathfrak p A_{\\mathfrak p}$.', 'Prime ideals of the localization correspond to primes of $A$ contained in $\\mathfrak p$. The extension of $\\mathfrak p$ contains every such prime and is therefore the unique maximal ideal.'), ('Residue field identification', 'The residue field of $A_{\\mathfrak p}$ is canonically isomorphic to the fraction field of $A/\\mathfrak p$.', 'Modulo $\\mathfrak p A_{\\mathfrak p}$, numerators and denominators descend to $A/\\mathfrak p$, and every denominator outside $\\mathfrak p$ becomes a nonzero class, hence invertible in the fraction field.')]}}
